ut does it eat the cat?The cat eats the dog (statement 4), but there\'s no mention of the tiger eating the cat.So unless another rule implies that, maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, the mouse eats the tiger (statement 8), so the tiger is being eaten by the mouse.But that might not affect the tiger\'s chasing behavior.\n\nHmm, perhaps we need to look at the other conditionals again.\n\nLooking at statement 19 again: If the tiger is green and big, then it sees the dog.We don\'t know if the tiger is green or big.The dog is big (statement 5), but the tiger\'s size isn\'t stated.However, maybe there\'s another way to infer the tiger is big?.\n\nLooking at statement 14: If something sees the mouse then it is big.So if the tiger sees the mouse, then the tiger must be big.But do we know if the tiger sees the mouse?Let\'s see.\n\nThe mouse does not see the tiger (statement 9), but that\'s the mouse\'s perspective.The tiger seeing the mouse isn\'t mentioned.So unless there\'s a rule that requires it, we can\'t assume.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the tiger does see the mouse, which would make it big, but that\'s speculative.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the dog sees the cat (statement 6), so the dog is seeing the cat.But statement 14 says that if something sees the mouse, then it\'s big.Since the dog is big (statement 5), but the dog isn\'t necessarily seeing the mouse.Unless there\'s a connection.\n\nWait, let\'s see if any of the statements force the tiger to be big.Suppose the tiger is big.Then, if it\'s also green, then statement 19 would apply.But since we don\'t know if it\'s green, that\'s still uncertain.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger is not big, so statement 19\'s condition isn\'t met, but that doesn\'t help us know if it sees the dog.\n\nHmm, perhaps another angle.Let\'s see if any of the existing facts can be connected to the tiger seeing the dog.\n\nLooking at statement 10: The tiger eats the dog.If something eats the dog, does that imply it can see the dog?Not necessarily