st not be red), the bear must not be red. But the facts mention the bear\'s color. So that\'s another inferred fact: the bear is not red.\n\nDon\'t wait, this is not a key point. Because the bear now doesn\'t need the tiger (due to rule D), so rule E applies, meaning the bear must not be red. But the problem\'s facts didn\'t state the bear\'s color. So that\'s a new non-implication.\n\nSo, now the bear must not be red. The rabbit must not be red (from not needing the tiger via rule A), the bald eagle is not red (fact 2), and the bear is not red (from rule E via rule D).\n\nNow, the tiger\'s color is still mentioned. The only colors not mentioned are red and blue. Since the question is not about the tiger being blue, perhaps there\'s no way to see if that\'s possible or not.\n\nDon\'t see: If the tiger is not blue, then according to rule G, it doesn\'t visit the bear, which it does (fact 14), so that\'s not okay.\n\nBut don\'t see if the bear\'s inferred redness affects anything. The bear is not red. The rules say something about red things except that if something doesn\'t need the tiger, it must not be red (rule E).\n\nNow, couldn\'t the tiger be blue? Don\'t see if any rule would conflict with that.\n\nSuppose the tiger is not blue. Then, since the tiger is not blue and doesn\'t need the bald eagle (fact 12), does that not trigger any rule? Rule H says if something is not blue and doesn\'t need the tiger, then it must visit the bald eagle. But here, the tiger doesn\'t need the bald eagle (fact 12), so the tiger not needing the bald eagle doesn\'t make it need the tiger. Therefore, rule H\'s condition is met here. The tiger\'s own needs affect rule H because the rule is about needing the tiger, not needing others.\n\nAnother angle: The rabbit must not be red (from rule E and being small). The rabbit doesn\'t visit the tiger (fact 11). If the tiger were not blue, then the rabbit is not visiting a blue animal, and the rules don\'t have anything about not visiting blu