an\'t infer that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rabbit is seen by someone else.Let\'s see: .\n\nLooking at premise 10: the squirrel sees the cat..\n\nPremise 4: cow sees the cat..\n\nPremise 6: rabbit sees the cat..\n\nSo all three (squirrel, cow, rabbit) see the cat..\n\nBut who sees the rabbit?.\n\nThe only possible candidates might be the squirrel, because it eats the rabbit.But unless that\'s implied, we can\'t say.Alternatively, maybe the rabbit is seen by the squirrel?Or not..\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the rabbit is being seen by the squirrel?The problem doesn\'t state that..\n\nWait, premise 12: If the rabbit is kind and the rabbit sees the squirrel then the squirrel needs the rabbit.But the rabbit\'s seeing the squirrel is part of the antecedent here.Does the rabbit see the squirrel?The premises don\'t say that..\n\nLooking at all the "sees" actions:.\n\n- Cat sees the squirrel (premise 2).\n- Cow sees the cat (premise 4).\n- Rabbit sees the cat (premise 6).\n- Squirrel sees the cat (premise 10).\n- Squirrel sees the cat again (maybe premise 10 is the same as another?Wait, the problem lists the squirrel sees the cat as premise 10. .\n\nSo the squirrel\'s actions are: eats the rabbit (premise7), is cold (8), needs the rabbit (9), sees the cat (10)..\n\nNo mention of the squirrel seeing the rabbit..\n\nSo, the only entity that might be seeing the rabbit is someone else..\n\nWait, perhaps the squirrel, since it eats the rabbit, but unless eating requires seeing, we can\'t assume that..\n\nAlternatively, the cow: does the cow see the rabbit?The cow sees the cat (premise4), but no mention of the rabbit..\n\nHmm, so maybe no one else is seeing the rabbit except perhaps the rabbit itself?But the rabbit sees the cat, not itself..\n\nWait, unless the rabbit is seeing itself?But that\'s not stated..\n\nAlternatively, maybe the cat sees the rabbit?The cat eats the squirrel (premise1), but does it see the rabbit?.\n\nWait, the cat\'s actions: eats the squirrel (1), sees t