chases the squirrel then the squirrel visits the cat..\n\nDoes anyone chase the squirrel?Not mentioned, unless inferred..\n\nStatement 21: If the cow is rough then the cow chases the cat.The cow\'s color is blue (5), but is it rough?The lion is rough (10).The cow\'s roughness isn\'t stated.So this condition might not apply.\n\nBack to the question: does the cat like the lion?The direct statements only mention the cat likes the cow.Are there any implications?.\n\nLooking at statement 15: If something likes the squirrel and it is big, then the squirrel likes the lion..\n\nThe squirrel likes the cow (12), but does it like the squirrel?The squirrel\'s self-liking isn\'t mentioned.Wait, the first part requires "something likes the squirrel".The squirrel is liked by.. the problem doesn\'t state anything else liking the squirrel except maybe the cat?Wait, the cat\'s likes are only the cow.Unless the cat likes the squirrel, but that\'s not stated..\n\nAlternatively, maybe the cat is big (1), and if the cat likes the squirrel, then since it\'s big, the squirrel would like the lion.But the cat\'s liking the squirrel isn\'t given.So this might not help.\n\nLooking at statement 16: If something likes the cow and it visits the cow, then the cow visits the cat..\n\nThe cat likes the cow (3), but does the cat visit the cow?The cat visits the lion (4), not the cow.So that condition isn\'t met..\n\nStatement 18: If something chases the lion and the lion is not cold then it visits the cow..\n\nThe lion is not cold?Wait, the cat is cold (2), but the lion\'s temperature isn\'t stated.Wait, the problem says "the lion is not big" (9) and "the lion is rough" (10), but nothing about the lion\'s temperature.So the lion could be cold or not.So this condition\'s second part (lion not cold) is unknown.Also, who is chasing the lion?Not mentioned unless inferred.\nI don\'t think it\'s possible to infer that the cat dislikes the lion.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
