g, so Dave is big. Now, looking at rule 20: \"All rough, big things are smart.\" Dave is rough and big, so that makes him smart, which we already had. But does that lead to anything else?\n\nAlternatively, is there a rule that if something is big and smart, then not quiet? Not that I can see. Since Gary is big (as he's round \u2192 big?), wait, Gary is round (point 10), so by point 21 he is big. Gary is also smart (point 11), so big and smart, yet he is quiet (point 9). Therefore, being big and smart doesn't prevent quietness. So no.\n\nTherefore, I think there's no way to determine whether Dave is quiet or not. Thus, the answer is unknown, which is C.\n\nWait a moment. Wait, the question is \"Dave is not quiet\" \u2014 so to say that the statement is true, we have to prove Dave is not quiet. To say it's false, prove he is quiet. Since we can't, it's unknown. So the answer must be C.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any explicit mention that Dave is not quiet. The premises don't say anything about Dave's quietness except that others are quiet or not. Since only Gary is mentioned as quiet, but that's the only mention. The question is about Dave, so since there's no info, it's unknown. Therefore, the answer is C.\n\nHmm, but I'm wondering if there's a hidden contradiction here. Let me see: is there any rule that would require Dave to be quiet? If not, then the answer remains C.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps since Gary is the only one mentioned as quiet, and Dave isn't listed as quiet, but maybe that's an oversight. But logical reasoning questions usually don't consider what's not stated unless there's a rule excluding it.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the \"quiet\" is a unique attribute? Since only Gary is called quiet, maybe others are not. But logically, that's an assumption. Unless there's a rule that says \"only Gary is quiet,\" but the premises don't state that. So it's possible that others can be quiet or not. So the answer is unknown. So I think the answer is C.\n\nWait, but wait! Let me check again the list of Dave's attributes. Let me list all that we've deduced so far from the premises:\n\nDave is furry (given), so rough (from 16), so rough and furry \u2192 smart (13), so smart \u2192 white (15), so white. Then white and furry \u2192 round (18), round \u2192 big (21), so big. Also, since he's rough and big, he's smart (20), which is consistent.\n\nSo Dave has the attributes: furry, rough, smart, white, round, big. Are there any other attributes? Not that I can see.\n\nNow, is there any rule that says if someone is big and round and furry, then they can't be quiet? For instance, maybe the \"quiet\" attribute is only for those with certain traits, but there's no such rule. Since Gary is big (round \u2192 big), round, smart, white, and quiet, so quietness can coexist with those traits. Therefore, Dave could be quiet or not quiet. There's nothing in the rules that says that quietness is incompatible with his attributes, so the answer remains unknown.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be option C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but wait again, maybe there's a rule that states something else? Let me recheck all the rules:\n\nLooking at the rules:\n\n- Rule 13: Rough, furry \u2192 smart. (applies to Dave, so he's smart)\n- Rule 14: Rough and round \u2192 smart. (Dave is round, so if he's rough (which he is), then smart, which he is)\n- Rule 15: If Dave is smart \u2192 white. (he is)\n- Rule 16: Furry \u2192 rough. (applies to Dave)\n- Rule 17: Round and rough \u2192 smart. (same as 14)\n- Rule 18: White and furry \u2192 round. (applies to Dave)\n- Rule 19: White and big \u2192 round. (Dave is white and big, so must be round, which he is)\n- Rule 20: Rough and big \u2192 smart. (applies to Dave)\n- Rule 21: Round \u2192 big. (applies to Dave)\n\nNone of these rules link to quietness, except for Gary's explicit mention.\n\nSo yeah, conclusion is C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but the question is: \"is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Dave is 