ake. Because premise 12 says furry \u2192 green, and since red \u2192 furry, then red \u2192 furry \u2192 green. So all red things are green. So red things are green. Therefore, Charlie is green. Therefore the statement is false.\n\nThe earlier confusion came from misinterpreting premise 9 and 12. Let me clarify:\n\nPremise 9: All furry things are red \u2192 furry \u2192 red.\n\nPremise 8: All red things are furry \u2192 red \u2192 furry.\n\nThus, red \u2194 furry.\n\nPremise 12: All furry things are green \u2192 furry \u2192 green.\n\nTherefore, red \u2194 furry \u2192 green. So red \u2192 green. So all red things are green.\n\nTherefore, Charlie is red \u2192 green. So he's green, so \"not green\" is false \u2192 B.\n\nThe premise 13 says if something is red and green (which Charlie is), then it must be blue. So Charlie is also blue. So he has all three? That's allowed unless there's a rule against it. Since the premises don't prohibit it, it's okay. Therefore, Charlie is green, so the answer is B.\n\nWait, the key mistake earlier was thinking \"red things are not green\", but actually they are green because of the premises. So the correct answer is B) False.\n\nTherefore, my initial conclusion was correct but got confused in the middle. So the answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is **B) False**.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Charlie is young (given)** \u2192 By premise 10 (\"Young things are red\"), Charlie is red.\n2. **Red \u2192 furry (premise 8)** \u2192 Charlie is furry.\n3. **Furry \u2192 green (premise 12)** \u2192 Charlie is green.\n4. **Red \u2227 green \u2192 blue (premise 13)** \u2192 Charlie must also be blue. However, this does not negate his status as green.\n5. **Conclusion:** Charlie is green. Therefore, the statement \"Charlie is not green\" is **false**.\n\n**Key Points:**\n- All red things are furry (premise 8) and all furry things are red (premise 9), forming an equivalence between red and furry.\n- Furry things are green (premise 12), so red things are inherently green.\n- Charlie\u2019s greenness is unavoidable based on his youth \u2192 red \u2192 furry \u2192 green chain.\n- The \"blue\" attribute from premise 13 does not conflict with being green; it adds another color but doesn\u2019t negate existing properties.\n\nThus, **B) False** is correct.",
