o that doesn't help.\n\nNow, the question is about the lion. Since there's no info about the lion seeing the dog or not, so the lion could or couldn't visit. Thus, the answer is C.\n\nWait, but maybe the dog's seeing the lion (statement 6) is relevant? Not directly. \n\nAlternatively, the bear seeing the dog would require the bear to visit itself, but that's okay.\n\nHmm. I think I've gone through all possible paths and there's no way to determine the lion's visit to the bear, so the answer must be C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but let me check the options again. The options are:\n\nA) True\n\nB) False\n\nC) Unknown\n\nThe question is whether the statement \"The lion does not visit the bear\" is true. If we can't confirm it's true or false, it's unknown. So yes, C.\n\nWait, but I recall that in logic puzzles like this, sometimes you have to consider that if there's a path that makes it possible, but also possible for it not to be, then it's unknown. So yes, I think C is correct.\n\nBut wait, let me check another angle. Suppose someone claims that the lion cannot visit the bear because of some rule.\n\nWait, could there be a rule that prevents the lion from visiting? Let me see the statements again.\n\nNo, the only rules are the conditionals. There's no explicit rule against the lion visiting the bear.\n\nAlternatively, if the lion visits the bear, then the bear must see the dog (from statement 18 via the lion seeing the bear). Now, if the bear sees the dog, then by statement 21, the bear must visit the bear, which is allowed. There's no contradiction in that. So the lion visiting is possible.\n\nBut since there's no requirement for the lion to do so, then the statement \"the lion does not visit\" could be true or false. Hence unknown.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C.\n\nWait, but I might be overcomplicating. Let me try to see if there's any way the lion must visit the bear. Suppose the lion sees the dog, then it must visit. But we don't know if the lion sees the dog. So it cou