      "text": " Gary must not be smart, so the statement is true, so answer A.\n\nWait, but in the first analysis I thought that being young and red would require him to be smart. But if Gary is cold, which requires him to be white, then white makes him young, so he is young. Then since he is red and young, he must be smart. But then that leads to contradictions.\n\nTherefore, the problem must not allow that, so there must be an inconsistency in assuming Gary is smart, therefore the only possible conclusion is that Gary is not smart, so the statement is true. So the answer is A) True.\n\nWait, but if the rules can't be satisfied unless Gary is not smart, then the correct answer is A.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I messed up the steps.\n\nLet me try again:\n\nGary is cold \u2192 white (rule 15). Thus, Gary is white.\n\nWhite \u2192 young (rule 14). Thus, Gary is young.\n\nGary is red (given), so he is young and red \u2192 must be smart (rule 17).\n\nBut if Gary is smart, then he must be green (rule 11).\n\nGreen \u2192 nice (rule 12). Thus, Gary must be nice.\n\nBut the problem states no information about Gary's niceness, but that's okay unless there is a contradiction.\n\nHowever, Gary is also white (from coldness). Now, if he is green and smart, then rule 16 says he must be white. Which is okay since he's already white.\n\nSo that's consistent. So there's no contradiction here.\n\nWait, then why did I think there was a contradiction before? Maybe I was mistaken.\n\nWait, being green and red: The problem doesn't say anything about green and red being incompatible. So maybe that's allowed.\n\nThus, if Gary is smart, he must be green and nice, but there is no rule against being green and red. So that's possible.\n\nTherefore, Gary could be smart, but then the question is whether that is possible or required?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nGary is young (from cold \u2192 white \u2192 young). Also, Gary is red. So by rule 17, all young red people are smart. So Gary must be smart. Therefore, he is smart.\n\nIf he is smart, then he must be green (rule 11). That's okay.\n\nThen, since he is green and smart, rule 16 requires him to be white, which he is.\n\nAnd being green makes him nice (rule12). So Gary must be nice. But the problem didn't say Gary is nice, but the rules don't prohibit him from being nice. So that's acceptable.\n\nTherefore, there's no contradiction. So Gary must be smart, so the statement \"Gary is not smart\" is false, so the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but then my previous thought process had conflicting conclusions. I must reconcile.\n\nSo let's try to summarize step by step:\n\n1. Gary is cold \u2192 must be white (rule15). So white.\n\n2. White \u2192 young (rule14). So Gary is young.\n\n3. Gary is red (given). So he is young and red \u2192 by rule17, must be smart. So smart.\n\nTherefore, Gary must be smart. Therefore, the statement \"Gary is not smart\" is false. So the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, so where did I go wrong before? Initially, I thought that being cold \u2192 white, but maybe I confused the direction? No, rule15 is cold \u2192 white. So Gary must be white.\n\nThen, being smart requires him to be green (rule11). But there is no rule saying green and red can't coexist. So green and red are okay.\n\nThus, the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but wait, the problem says \"All young, red people are smart\" (rule17). Since Gary is young (from white) and red, he must be smart. Therefore, he is smart. Therefore, \"Gary is not smart\" is false. So answer B.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be B) False.\n\nBut then why did I think there was a contradiction before? Because I thought that being white requires him to be young (which is okay) and then being smart would require green and that would make him nice, but that's okay unless there's a rule that says being nice must be only young, but the rules say \"Nice people are young\" (rule13), which is nice \u2192 young, which is okay because if he's nice, then he is young (which he is). So no problem.\n\nSo the conclusion is that Gary must be smart, so the statement is false. So B.\n\nWait, but wait another angle: Fiona is not red (given). The rule 18 says if Fiona is red, then green. Since Fiona is not red, that's irrelevant.\n\nAnne is nice and young. If Anne is smart, then she's not green (rule19). But Anne's smartness is not given, so she might or might not be.\n\nDave is smart, so he must be green (rule11). Dave must also be nice (rule12). The context says Dave is nice, so that's okay. Also, Dave is white, so he must be young (rule14), but the context says Dave is not explicitly young? Wait, the context says:\n\nWait, looking back at the initial context:\n\nWait the context says:\n\nAnne is nice. Anne is young.\n\nDave is cold. Dave is smart. Dave is white.\n\nFiona is nice. Fiona is not red. Fiona is young.\n\nGary is cold. Gary is red.\n\nWait, the context does not explicitly say Dave is young, but according to rule14 (white \u2192 young), since Dave is white, he must be young. So Dave is young, which is okay.\n\nSo putting it all together, Gary must be smart, so the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"If someone is green and smart then they are white.\" (rule16). Gary is smart and green, so must be white. He is white, so that's okay.\n\nTherefore, all rules are satisfied if Gary is smart. Therefore, the statement \"Gary is not smart\" is false, so answer B.\n\nSo my initial mistake was thinking that there was a contradiction, but there isn't. Therefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but wait another thing: Fiona is young (given), Fiona is not red (given), so she's not covered by rule17, so her smartness isn't enforced. So Fiona could be smart or not.\n\nSo in conclusion, Gary must be smart, so the answer is B.\n\nHmm, I think this is the correct path. Initially I made a mistake in thinking there was a contradiction, but upon re-examining, there's no contradiction. So the answer must be B.\n</think>\n\nGary is cold, so by rule 15 (Cold \u2192 White), he must be white. Being white implies he is young (rule 14). Since Gary is both young and red (given), rule 17 (Young \u2227 Red \u2192 Smart) compels him to be smart. Thus, the statement \"Gary is not smart\" is **false**. \n\n**Answer:** B) False",
