 and blue, perhaps being blue and white requires him to be big? But the rule is one-way. The rule says if white and big, then blue. It doesn't say the converse. So blue and white doesn't imply big. So that's not helpful.\n\nFact 18: Blue people are cold. Which he is.\n\nFact 16: Blue implies green, which he is.\n\nFact 12: Young people are white. So if he were young, then he must be white, which he is. But again, that doesn't force him to be young.\n\nAnother angle: Let's see if there are any contradictions if we assume he is big or not big.\n\nAssume Dave is big. Then, since he is big and white (fact 15), he must be blue, which he is. So no contradiction here.\n\nAssume Dave is not big. Is there any contradiction? Let's see.\n\nIf he's not big, then does any rule apply?\n\nFact 17: If he were young and white, then big. But since he's not big, that would require that he is not young or not white. But he is white, so he must not be young. But we can't know that from the given info. So that's possible.\n\nAlternatively, are there any other rules that would require him to be big if not?\n\nChecking all rules again:\n\nFact 15: no problem.\n\nFact 17: only applies if young.\n\nFact 12: only applies if young.\n\nFact 13: If he were young and cold, then rough. But since he's cold, if he were young, he must be rough. But his roughness isn't stated. The problem doesn't say Dave is rough, so maybe he isn't. But according to the rule, if he were young and cold, he must be rough. So if he is young but not rough, that would contradict. But since we don't know his roughness, we can't confirm that. So this doesn't help.\n\nThus, both possibilities (Dave being big or not big) are consistent with all the given rules and premises. Therefore, there's insufficient information to determine whether Dave is big or not. Hence the statement \"Dave is not big\" would be unknown because we can't confirm it's true or false.\n\nWait, but let me think again. Maybe there's a rule I missed that can connect being blue, white, and green to being big?\n\nLooking at the rules again:\n\nFact 15: White and big \u2192 blue. But since he's already blue and white, but being big would just make that statement hold, but it doesn't require him to be big.\n\nFact 16: All blue are green, so that's already satisfied.\n\nFact 18: Blue \u2192 cold. Also satisfied.\n\nAnother possible angle: The problem says \"White, big people are blue.\" So the set of white and big people is a subset of blue people. But since Dave is white and blue, he could be in that subset (if big), or outside (if not big). But there's no rule that requires white and blue to be big.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"White, big people are blue\" could be rephrased as \"If someone is white and big, then they are blue,\" which is the way the problem states it. So that doesn't impose any requirement on white and blue people to be big.\n\nHmm. I think I've covered all possibilities. Since both scenarios (Dave being big or not) are possible without violating any rules, the correct answer is unknown. So the statement \"Dave is not big\" could be true or false, so the answer is C) Unknown.\n</think>\n\nC) Unknown\n\n### Step-by-Step Explanation:\n1. **Dave's Attributes**: From the context, Dave is **blue** and **white** (facts 8 and 9).  \n2. **Derived Attributes**:  \n   - **Green**: All blue people are green (fact 16), so Dave is green.  \n   - **Cold**: Blue people are cold (fact 18), so Dave is cold.  \n\n3. **Rules Involving \"Big\"**:  \n   - **Fact 15**: \"White, big people are blue.\" This means if Dave were big, his being blue is consistent, but it doesn\u2019t force him to be big.  \n   - **Fact 17**: \"If someone is young and white, then they are big.\" Dave is white, but his age is unknown. If he were young, he must be big, but since his youth is unconfirmed, this rule doesn\u2019t apply.  \n\n4. **Checking Contradictions**:  \n   - **Assuming Dave is big**: No contradictions arise (all attributes align with given rules).  \n   - **Assuming Dave is not big**: Also no contradictions. His attributes (blue, white, cold, green) don\u2019t violate any rules, and his age remains unknown.  \n\n5. **Conclusion**: Since both scenarios (Dave being big or not) are logically consistent with the premises, there\u2019s insufficient information to confirm or deny the statement \"Dave is not big.\" Thus, the answer is **unknown** (Option C).",
