s young, is there a rule that would require something else?\n\nAlternatively, let's look for rules that can lead to being not young. But the rules don't state that, unless...\n\nWait, the only rules that mention Y (young) are:\n\nRule 3: If (Y \u2227 \u00acN) \u2192 O. But O is already required by other rules (if she's white, she must be round). \n\nRule 5: If (Y \u2227 G) \u2192 S. But Fiona isn't green.\n\nRule 8: R \u2192 Y. So if Fiona is rough, then she is young. But if she is not rough, then we don't know from that.\n\nAlternatively, is there a rule that says if \u00acY, then something? Not directly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Fiona's whiteness and roundness could be linked to other attributes.\n\nWait, Fiona is white and round. Let me think of all possible ways.\n\nSince Fiona is white, she is already round (rule 2). \n\nIf we can find any rule that requires Fiona to be young or not, but I can't see any. Let me check each rule:\n\nRule 1: N \u2192 O. Fiona isn't nice (unless stated). The context only says Fiona is white, so unless another rule makes her nice. But no, the context only lists her as white. So she's not nice. So that's okay.\n\nRule 2: W \u2192 O, which is satisfied.\n\nRule 3: If (Y \u2227 \u00acN) \u2192 O. Fiona is \u00acN (since she's not nice), so if she were Y (young), then she must be O (round), which she is. But this doesn't give us info about her being young or not. It just says if she is Y and \u00acN, then she must be O. Since she is O, that part holds, but that doesn't help us determine Y.\n\nRule 4: Already handled for Bob.\n\nRule 5: If (Y and G) \u2192 S. Fiona isn't G, so irrelevant.\n\nRule 6: S \u2192 W. Fiona isn't stated to be smart, so okay.\n\nRule 7: If (R \u2227 \u00acG) \u2192 W. If Fiona were rough and not green, then she must be white, which she is. Since she is white, that could be satisfied, but since she's white anyway, doesn't tell us about being rough.\n\nRule 8: R \u2192 Y. So if Fiona is rough, then she must be young. But we don't know if she's rough.\n\nAlternatively, is there any rule that would require Fiona to be rough?\n\nRule 7 says if something is rough and not green, then it's white. Since Fiona is white, but not necessarily rough or not. \n\nAlternatively, if Fiona is not rough, then does that affect anything? Not directly. \n\nWait, maybe we can see if Fiona's not being rough and not young would violate any rules?\n\nSuppose Fiona is not young (the statement claims she's not young, so we need to see if that can be true, false, or unknown).\n\nAssume Fiona is not young (\u00acY). Does that lead to a contradiction?\n\nIf Fiona is \u00acY (not young), then can we derive any contradictions?\n\nLet me see. If she's not young, then what rules are affected?\n\nLooking at the rules:\n\nRule 3: (Y \u2227 \u00acN) \u2192 O. The contrapositive would be if \u00acO \u2192 \u00ac(Y \u2227 \u00acN). Since O is true (she's round), this doesn't help.\n\nRule 8: R \u2192 Y. If she were rough (R), then she must be Y. If she's \u00acY, then she can't be rough. So if Fiona is \u00acY, then she must not be rough (\u00acR). \n\nBut being \u00acR doesn't contradict anything, unless another rule requires her to be rough. \n\nAlternatively, is there a rule that says if \u00acY and some other condition, then something must be true?\n\nAlternatively, maybe Fiona's attributes don't give enough info to determine her age. Let's think of possible scenarios:\n\nCase 1: Fiona is young (Y). Is that possible?\n\nIf Fiona is Y, then she could be, but there's no rule that forces her to be Y unless she has other attributes. Since she's white and round, but not rough, she could be young or not.\n\nWait, if she's young, does that require anything else?\n\nFor example, if she's young and not nice (which she isn't), then by rule 3, she must be round, which she is. So that's okay. So being Y doesn't cause a problem.\n\nCase 2: Fiona is not young (\u00acY). That also doesn't conflict with any rules, as far as I