t does not eat the lion (statement 4). The cat sees the mouse (statement 5), the lion sees the cat (statement 6), the mouse sees the bald eagle (statement 9), and the mouse sees the cat (statement 10).\n\nWait, but none of these statements influence the lion's roughness except statement 13. Since there's no further info, the only way to have a definite answer is if we can prove that the lion must be rough or must not be. Since the only thing we know is that if not rough, then cold, but cold is possible, so we can't say for sure. Therefore, it's unknown, so the answer is C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but hold on! Wait, perhaps there's an indirect contradiction here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the lion being green (from earlier steps) \u2013 but does being green have any other implications? Statement 18 says that if someone is green, they like the bald eagle. We already accounted for that. The lion is green, so it likes the bald eagle, which is okay.\n\nAlternatively, if the lion were not rough, then it's cold. But there's nothing that says being cold is impossible. So there's no contradiction in the lion being not rough and cold. Therefore, since both possibilities (rough or not rough) don't lead to contradictions, we can't determine it's true or false. Therefore, the answer must be unknown.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't miss anything. Let me check all the premises again.\n\nWait statement 7 says the mouse eats the lion. So the mouse is eating the lion, and the lion eats the mouse (statement 16). That's an interesting loop. But does that affect anything else?\n\nWell, the mouse is kind because the lion eats the mouse (statement 12), leading to the mouse liking the lion (statement 15), which makes the lion green (statement14). The greenness makes the lion like the bald eagle (statement 18). The bald eagle doesn't like the lion (statement 2) but the lion likes the bald eagle. That's okay, since it's mutual feelings can be different.\n\nSo no contradiction there.\n\nH