g, so she can be smart without the contradiction? Or maybe she can't be smart at all unless she is young, but we don't know her age.\n\nHowever, none of this affects her color. The premises explicitly state Fiona is white, so unless there's a rule that invalidates that, the answer is B) False (since the statement claims she is not white, which contradicts the given info). Therefore, the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, but the question is phrased as \"Based on the above information...\", so if the information includes Fiona is white, then the answer is straightforward. But perhaps the problem is a trick question where some rule overrides that?\n\nWait, let me check all the premises again carefully.\n\nLooking at rule 8: Furry, nice things are white. But that doesn't mean that white things must be furry and nice. So Fiona being white doesn't have to be furry or nice unless other conditions apply.\n\nAnother point: Rule 16 says if something is nice, it is blue. Fiona is white and blue, so that's okay. If Fiona is nice, then she must be blue (which she is), but she can still be white as well unless there's a rule against it.\n\nWait, maybe the color attributes are exclusive? The problem doesn't state that. So being both blue and white is allowed.\n\nTherefore, Fiona is definitely white, so the statement \"Fiona is not white\" is false, so the answer is B) False.\n\nHmm, but why is there an option C? Maybe I missed a contradiction?\n\nLet me think of another angle. Suppose Fiona is smart.\n\nIf Fiona is smart, then since she is blue (4), by rule 10 (blue and smart implies furry), so she must be furry. Also, since she is white and smart, by rule 11 (smart and white implies furry), so again furry. So she must be furry.\n\nBut then, is there any rule that would make her not furry? Rule 13 says if Fiona is smart AND young, then she is not furry. So if she is smart and young, that would require her to be both furry (from rules 10 and 11) and not furry (from rule 13), which is a contradiction. Therefore, to avoid contradiction, Fiona cannot be both smart and young. But unless she is young, this doesn't matter. Since her youth is not stated, she could be or not be young. So if she is not young, then being smart is okay, as she would be furry. But since we don't know her age, maybe her smartness is possible, but not necessary.\n\nAlternatively, is there any way Fiona could not be white? The premises state she is white, so unless there's a rule that would make that impossible, but I don't see any. Therefore, the answer must be B) False.\n\nWait a second! Wait, the problem says \"Based on the above information...\", which includes all the given premises. So since premise 5 says Fiona is white, the statement \"Fiona is not white\" directly contradicts that, so it's false. Therefore the correct answer is B) False. The other premises may add more info, but nothing contradicts premise 5. So the answer is B.\n\nBut why would the question be in the problem set then? Maybe I made a mistake in reading the premises.\n\nWait, let me recheck the premises again:\n\nContext:\n\nCharlie is young.\n\nErin is blue.\n\nErin is not kind.\n\nFiona is blue.\n\nFiona is white.\n\nGary is blue.\n\nGary is kind.\n\nFurry, nice things are white.\n\nIf something is young and blue then it is smart.\n\nAll blue, smart things are furry.\n\nAll smart, white things are furry.\n\nYoung things are nice.\n\nIf Fiona is smart and Fiona is young then Fiona is not furry.\n\nIf Erin is kind then Erin is furry.\n\nIf Gary is smart and Gary is white then Gary is not kind.\n\nIf something is nice then it is blue.\n\nYes, Fiona is explicitly stated as white (premise 5). So the answer is B) False because the statement claims Fiona is not white, which contradicts the given info.\n\nWait, maybe I misread the question? The question is asking if \"Fiona is not white\" is true, false, or unknown. Since it's given that Fiona is white, the statement is false, so answer is B. Therefore,