 the correct answer is B) False. I think I made a mistake earlier when I thought it was unknown, but no, the reasoning leads to B.\n</think>\n\nB) False\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n1. **Given Charlie's Attributes**: Charlie is smart (statement 4) and nice (statement 3).\n2. **Inference from Smart to Young**: Since all smart people are young (statement 13), Charlie must be young.\n3. **Conflict with Statement 12**: \n   - Statement 12 states: *\"If someone is round and nice, they are not young.\"*\n   - Charlie is young (from step 2) and nice (given). \n   - If Charlie were round, he would satisfy \"round \u2227 nice,\" which would require him to be *not young*. This contradicts his established youth.\n4. **Conclusion**: Charlie cannot be round. Thus, the statement \"Charlie is round\" is **False**.\n\n**Answer:** B) False",
