s not young. Harry is green. If Gary is green then Gary is not blue. All young people are blue. If Harry is white and Harry is young then Harry is not green. All red people are green. Blue, young people are red. If someone is green then they are white. If someone is blue and white then they are kind. All red people are not rough. If someone is young and not white then they are rough.\n\nCounting these, there are 16 statements. So my previous numbering was off by one. Let me list them properly:\n\n1. Anne is young.\n\n2. Charlie is not red.\n\n3. Gary is not blue.\n\n4. Gary is kind.\n\n5. Gary is not rough.\n\n6. Gary is not young.\n\n7. Harry is green.\n\n8. If Gary is green then Gary is not blue.\n\n9. All young people are blue.\n\n10. If Harry is white and Harry is young then Harry is not green.\n\n11. All red people are green.\n\n12. Blue, young people are red.\n\n13. If someone is green then they are white.\n\n14. If someone is blue and white then they are kind.\n\n15. All red people are not rough.\n\n16. If someone is young and not white then they are rough.\n\nAh, so statement 16 is the last one: \"If someone is young and not white then they are rough.\"\n\nOkay, so now back to Anne.\n\nAnne is young (1), so she is blue (9). Since she is blue and young (from 9 and 1), by 12, she must be red. Therefore, Anne is red. Since all red people are not rough (15), she cannot be rough. So that would answer B) False.\n\nBut wait, there's another angle: statement 16 says that if someone is young and not white, then they are rough. So if Anne is not white, then she would be rough. But we have to check if Anne is white or not.\n\nSince Anne is red (from above), and all red people are green (11), so Anne is green. Then by statement 13 (if someone is green, they are white), Anne must be white. Therefore, Anne is white. Thus, the condition in statement 16 (young and not white) doesn't apply to her. Therefore, Anne is not rough because she is red (so not rough by 15) and also because she's not in the \"young and not white\" category.\n\nTherefore, Anne is definitely not rough. So the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me double-check everything again to be sure.\n\nAnne's traits:\n\n- Young (1) \u2192 blue (9).\n\n- Since she's blue and young (as all young are blue), she must be red (12).\n\n- Red \u2192 green (11).\n\n- Green \u2192 white (13).\n\n- Therefore, she is white.\n\nNow, checking roughness:\n\n- Since she's red, she is not rough (15).\n\n- Also, she is young and white, so the only way she could be rough is if she were young and not white, but she is white, so that rule (16) doesn't affect her.\n\nTherefore, Anne is not rough. So the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but let me check Harry to see if there's any conflict. Harry is green (7), so by 13 he must be white. The statement 10 is: if Harry is white and young, then he can't be green. But Harry is white (from 13) and green, so if Harry were young, that would be a contradiction. Therefore, Harry cannot be young. So Harry's age is not young, which is okay because there's no statement saying he is young. So that's consistent.\n\nCharlie is not red (2). Since red people are green (11), Charlie can be any color except red, but that's okay.\n\nGary is not blue (3), is kind (4), not rough (5), not young (6). Since Gary is not young, his color isn't constrained by the young rules. But since he's not blue, and he might have other colors. But since he's not young, he could be any color except blue? But there's no info on his color except that he's not blue. Also, from the green and white rules, but unless he is green, which would make him white. But we don't know if Gary is green. The statement 8 says if Gary is green then he's not blue, which is true since he's already not blue. So that's okay. No contradictions there.\n\nSo everything seems consistent. Therefore, Anne cannot be rough. So the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nB) False\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Anne is young** (given).\n2. **All young people are blue** (statement 9 \u2192 Anne is blue).\n3. **Blue, young people are red** (statement 12 \u2192 since Anne is both blue and young, she must be red).\n4. **All red people are not rough** (statement 15 \u2192 as Anne is red, she cannot be rough).\n5. **Additional Check for Consistency:**\n   - Anne being red implies she\u2019s green (statement 11).\n   - Green \u2192 white (statement 13 \u2192 Anne is white).\n   - The rule \"If young and not white \u2192 rough\" (statement 16) doesn\u2019t apply to Anne because she is white.\n6. **Conclusion:** Anne cannot be rough. The statement \"Anne is rough\" is **False**. \n\nAnswer: **B) False**",
