point 15\'s condition isn\'t met, so we can\'t infer that the squirrel likes the lion..\n\nMoving on.Let\'s look at other implications.The squirrel likes the cow (point 12).Let\'s see if that triggers point 16. .\n\nPoint 16: If something (X) likes the cow and X visits the cow, then the cow visits the cat..\n\nSo, for X being the squirrel: does the squirrel like the cow (yes, point 12) and does the squirrel visit the cow?Well, the problem is we don\'t know if the squirrel visits the cow.The given info doesn\'t mention that.So unless we can infer that the squirrel visits the cow, this doesn\'t apply.Since we don\'t know that, maybe we can\'t use this..\n\nNext, looking at point 17: If something (X) likes the cat, then X chases the squirrel..\n\nDoes the squirrel like the cat?The squirrel likes the cow (point 12), but there\'s no info that the squirrel likes the cat.Unless there\'s a chain.Let\'s check:.\n\nThe cat likes the cow (point 3).The squirrel likes the cow (point 12).But does that mean the squirrel likes the cat?Not necessarily.Unless there\'s a transitive relation, which isn\'t stated.So, unless there\'s another rule, the squirrel\'s liking the cow doesn\'t translate to liking the cat.So the squirrel might not like the cat, so point 17\'s antecedent isn\'t met..\n\nPoint 18: If something (X) chases the lion and the lion is not cold, then X visits the cow..\n\nWe know the lion is not cold?Wait, the cat is cold (point 2), but the lion\'s temperature isn\'t mentioned.Wait, the lion is rough (point 10), but is there any info on the lion being cold or not?The problem states "the cat is cold," but nothing about the lion.So we don\'t know if the lion is cold or not.So this might be unknown..\n\nPoint 19: If something (X) chases the lion, then the lion chases the cow..\n\nSo if X chases the lion, then lion chases cow.But does the lion currently chase the cow?The lion chases the cat (point 8), but nothing about the cow.Unless someone is chasing the lion..\n\nPoint 20: If something (X) chases the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat..\n\nIf the squirrel is being chased by someone, then the squirrel would visit the cat.But does the squirrel ever get chased here?.\n\nPoint 21: If the cow is rough, then the cow chases the cat.The cow is blue (point 5), but is it rough?The lion is rough (point 10), but nothing about the cow.So the cow\'s roughness is unknown, so this might not apply..\n\nNow, the question is about whether the squirrel does not chase itself.So, does the squirrel chase the squirrel?Let\'s see if there\'s any rule that would cause that..\n\nFirst, let\'s see if the squirrel has any reason to chase itself..\n\nLooking at the implications, maybe through some chain.Let me think step by step.\n\nFirst, let\'s see if the squirrel does any action that could trigger a chase..\n\nThe squirrel likes the cow (point 12).Does that lead to anything?.\n\nLooking at point 15 again.Wait, maybe if the squirrel likes itself?But there\'s no info on that..\n\nAlternatively, perhaps if the squirrel visits someone, then some implication occurs..\n\nLooking at point 13: If something visits the squirrel, then it likes the cat..\n\nSuppose someone visits the squirrel.Then that visitor must like the cat.But we don\'t know if anyone visits the squirrel..\n\nAlternatively, if the squirrel visits someone, then we look at point 14: If something (X) visits the cat, then X likes the cat..\n\nBut if the squirrel visits the cat, then the squirrel must like the cat.However, do we know if the squirrel visits the cat?.\n\nWait, point 20 says if something chases the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat.So if the squirrel is chased by someone, then the squirrel would visit the cat.But does anyone chase the squirrel?.\n\nLooking at point 17: If someone (X) likes the cat, then X chases the squirrel..\n\nSo if someone likes the cat, they must chase the squirrel..\n\nDoes anyone like the cat?The cat likes the cow (point 3), but there\'s no info on others.The squirrel\'s feelings towards the cat are unknown..\n\nWait, the lion chases the cat (point 8), but does the lion like the cat?The problem doesn\'t say..\n\nWait, the lion is rough and not big.The lion visits the cat (point 11)..\n\nHmm.Let\'s see if there\'s a loop here..\n\nSuppose the squirrel starts chasing itself.Then, is there a rule that would make that possible or required?.\n\nAlternatively, maybe through another path.Let\'s see:.\n\nSuppose the squirrel chases the squirrel.Then, according to point 20, if something (the squirrel) chases the squirrel (itself), then the squirrel must visit the cat..\n\nSo if the squirrel chases itself, then the squirrel visits the cat..\n\nBut then, according to point 14, if something (the squirrel) visits the cat, then it (the squirrel) must like the cat..\n\nTherefore, if the squirrel chases itself, then two things happen: the squirrel visits the cat (from point 20), and therefore the squirrel must like the cat (from point 14)..\n\nBut does the squirrel like the cat?The problem only states that the squirrel likes the cow.So unless there\'s another rule, we don\'t know if the squirrel likes the cat..\n\nTherefore, if the squirrel were to chase itself, it would have to like the cat.But since we don\'t know if it does, maybe that\'s a contradiction?Wait, no.The implication is that if it visits the cat, then it must like the cat.So if the squirrel did chase itself, leading to visiting the cat, then the squirrel would have to like the cat.But if it doesn\'t like the cat, then it can\'t have visited the cat.So this creates a dependency..\n\nBut is there any other information that can link these?Let me think again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a way to derive that the squirrel must chase itself or cannot..\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if there\'s any existing actions that might lead to the squirrel chasing itself..\n\nThe squirrel likes the cow (point 12).Suppose the squirrel visits the cow.Then, according to point 16, if something (the squirrel) likes the cow and visits the cow, then the cow visits the cat..\n\nBut do we know if the squirrel visits the cow?Not directly.So that\'s an unknown..\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if the squirrel is being chased.Suppose someone else is chasing the squirrel, which would make the squirrel visit the cat (point 20).But who would be chasing the squirrel?.\n\nPoint 17 says if someone likes the cat, they must chase the squirrel.So if the squirrel likes the cat, then the squirrel would have to chase itself.Wait, that\'s interesting..\n\nWait, let\'s break that down..\n\nSuppose the squirrel likes the cat.Then, according to point 17, that would mean the squirrel (since it\'s the one liking the cat) must chase the squirrel (itself)..\n\nSo if the squirrel likes the cat, then squirrel chases squirrel..\n\nBut does the squirrel like the cat?The problem states that the squirrel likes the cow (point 12), but nothing about the cat.Unless there\'s a way to infer that the squirrel likes the cat..\n\nAlternatively, maybe through some other rule.Let\'s think..\n\nLooking at point 14: If something visits the cat, then it likes the cat..\n\nSuppose the squirrel visits the cat.Then the squirrel must like the cat..\n\nBut why would the squirrel visit the cat?That could happen if someone chases the squirrel (point 20)..\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel is forced to visit the cat through some other chain..\n\nAlternatively, if the squirrel is visiting the cat, then it must like the cat, but if it likes the cat, then by point 17, it must chase itself..\n\nThis creates a possible loop..\n\nLet me try to see if there\'s a possible scenario where the squirrel chases itself..\n\nSuppose the squirrel does like the cat.Then by point 17, it must chase itself..\n\nBut if it chases itself, then by point 20, the squirrel must visit the cat..\n\nVisiting the cat would then, via point 14, require that the squirrel likes the cat, which we assumed..\n\nSo in that case, it\'s consistent.But does the squirrel actually like the cat?The problem doesn\'t say..\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another path..\n\nWait, the squirrel\'s like for the cow might not be directly relevant here..\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel\'s chasing itself is impossible..\n\nWait, let\'s see.Let\'s suppose the squirrel does not like the cat.Then, point 17\'s condition isn\'t met, so there\'s no requirement for it to chase the squirrel.So in that case, the squirrel doesn\'t have to chase itself..\n\nBut then, the question is whether it\'s possible for the squirrel to chase itself, or if it\'s impossible, or if we don\'t know..\n\nThe problem is asking if "The squirrel does not chase the squirrel" is true, false, or unknown..\n\nSo to determine that, we need to see if the statements given allow us to conclude it must be true (so the answer is A), must be false (B), or if it\'s not determined (C)..\n\nTo see if it must be true: We need to show that there is no way the squirrel can chase itself, given the premises..\n\nTo see if it must be false: We need to show that the squirrel must chase itself..\n\nOtherwise, it\'s unknown..\n\nLet me try to see if there\'s any way the squirrel could chase itself..\n\nIf the squirrel likes the cat (even if that\'s not stated), then by point 17, it would have to chase itself.But since we don\'t know if the squirrel likes the cat, that\'s an unknown..\n\nAlternatively, could there be another reason for the squirrel to chase itself?.\n\nSuppose someone else is chasing the squirrel.Let\'s say the lion chases the squirrel..\n\nWait, does the lion chase anyone?The lion chases the cat (point 8).There\'s no mention of the lion chasing the squirrel..\n\nAlternatively, the cat might chase the squirrel?The cat\'s actions are visiting the lion, liking the cow, etc..\n\nAlternatively, maybe through another chain..\n\nWait, let\'s consider the following: .\n\nSuppose the squirrel does not like the cat.Then, there\'s no obligation for it to chase itself..\n\nBut could there be another reason for it to chase itself?.\n\nLooking at all the conditional statements, I don\'t see any other rules that would cause the squirrel to chase itself unless it likes the cat..\n\nTherefore, the only possible way the squirrel would chase itself is if it likes the cat.Since the problem doesn\'t state that the squirrel likes the cat, and there\'s no rule that requires it to, then we can\'t assume that..\n\nTherefore, the squirrel might or might not like the cat..\n\nIf it does, then it must chase itself (so the statement "does not chase" would be false)..\n\nIf it doesn\'t, then it doesn\'t have to chase itself (so the statement could be true)..\n\nSince the problem doesn\'t give us information about the squirrel\'s feelings toward the cat, we can\'t determine whether the squirrel does or doesn\'t like the cat..\n\nTherefore, the truth of the statement "The squirrel does not chase the squirrel" depends on whether the squirrel likes the cat or not, which is not provided in the premises..\n\nWait, but maybe there\'s another angle..\n\nLet me check if the squirrel could be forced to like the cat through some other rule..\n\nFor example, if the squirrel visits the cat, then it must like the cat (point 14)..\n\nBut why would the squirrel visit the cat?That could happen if someone is chasing the squirrel (point 20)..\n\nSuppose someone else is chasing the squirrel, causing it to visit the cat, which would then require it to like the cat..\n\nBut who would be chasing the squirrel?.\n\nPoint 17: If X likes the cat, then X chases the squirrel..\n\nSo if there exists an X that likes the cat, then that X would chase the squirrel..\n\nBut we don\'t know any X that likes the cat except possibly the squirrel itself..\n\nThe cat likes the cow (point 3), but not necessarily the cat..\n\nWait, the cat likes the cow, but there\'s no rule that the cat likes itself or others..\n\nThe only entity that is known to like the cat is possibly