gs are smart.So Harry can be smart without being quiet.So that\'s okay.Therefore, no problem.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that Gary is quiet.So the answer should be A) True.Wait, but wait another second.Let me check if there\'s any possibility that Gary isn\'t big.Because round things are big (premise 15), and Gary is round (from premise 12), so he must be big.So that\'s solid.So big and blue, so quiet.Therefore, yes.\nHmm, I don\'t need to check the options again.The options are A) Right, B) Incorrect, C) Known.So if all the premises do not lead us to conclude that Gary is quiet, then A is incorrect.But maybe I didn\'t miss anything?.\n\nGo ahead, maybe I made a correct assumption that being big and blue automatically applies to Gary.Let me proceed:.\n\nGary is blue (premise 3), white (4), so young (from blue via premise 10).Then, because young and white (premise 12), he\'s round.Round means big (premise 15).So big and blue (since he is blue), so big and blue things are quiet (premise 11).Therefore, no, not quiet.\n\nAlternatively, could there be no exception?The premises are all given as unconditional statements, so unless there\'s a consistent rule that says something else, this should not hold.Let me see if any other rules might not affect this.\n\nAnother angle: The question is about whether the statement is true, false, or known.If all the premises do not lead to a definite conclusion, then it\'s false or unknown.If there\'s a possibility that the premises give enough info, then known.\n\nBut in this case, none of the steps seem to be deductions from the premises.The premises do not form a chain that leads to Gary being quiet.Therefore, the answer should not be A) Right.But wait, maybe I didn\'t miss something.Let me check each step again.\n\n1.Gary is blue (premise 3) → young (premise 10).So not young.\n\n2.Gary is white (premise 4) + young (from above) → by premise 12 (young and white → round).So not round.\n\n3.Round → big (premise 15).So not big.\n\n4.