ng the mouse is needed, not seeing the bald eagle..\n\nIf the squirrel sees the mouse, then the squirrel needs the squirrel?Wait, if someone sees the mouse, then they need the squirrel.So if the squirrel sees the mouse, then squirrel needs the squirrel..\n\nWait, that\'s a possibility.Let me check:.\n\nSuppose the squirrel sees the mouse.Then, by statement 19, the squirrel would need the squirrel.But do we know if the squirrel sees the mouse?.\n\nWe need to see if the squirrel sees the mouse.The context says the mouse sees the bald eagle (statement 8).The bald eagle sees the mouse (from 17)..\n\nBut does the squirrel see the mouse?There\'s no direct statement about that.Unless another condition leads to that.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the squirrel is rough, but the squirrel is kind and nice, so probably not.So unless there\'s a way the squirrel is rough, which would require them to see the mouse (statement 17).But since the squirrel is not rough (as it\'s kind and nice), then 17 doesn\'t apply..\n\nSo the squirrel might or might not see the mouse.Since it\'s not stated, we can\'t assume.Therefore, unless there\'s another condition that forces the squirrel to see the mouse, we can\'t know..\n\nWait, but maybe another path.Let\'s think:.\n\nSuppose the squirrel needs the squirrel.Let\'s see if that can be derived..\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel needs the squirrel via some other rule.Let me see.\n\nLooking at statement 14: If someone is blue and sees the tiger, then the tiger needs the squirrel.But we don\'t know if the squirrel is blue, or if they see the tiger.The tiger is kind (statement 12)..\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel likes the tiger?Not sure..\n\nAlternatively, statement 16: If the mouse needs the squirrel, then the squirrel likes the mouse.But do we know if the mouse needs the squirrel?The mouse\'s needs are not stated except in statement 3 (bald eagle needs mouse).So maybe the mouse doesn\'t need the squirrel, so this implication\'s antecedent 