nTherefore, all the deductions are valid. So Bob must be nice.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Bob is not nice\" is false \u2192 answer B.\n\nHmm, so maybe I had a confusion earlier between smart and not smart.\n\nWait, another angle: Let me check the rules again.\n\nWait, all round things are smart \u2192 Bob is round \u2192 smart.\n\nSmart \u2192 furry (rule1).\n\nFurry \u2192 young (rule3).\n\nSo young \u2192 via rule6, nice.\n\nTherefore, all steps hold. So Bob must be nice. So the answer is B (False).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.\n\nWait but in the initial list of Bob's properties, the user says Bob is blue, round. There's no mention of nice. So according to the rules, he must be nice, so the statement that Bob is not nice would be false. So B would be the correct answer.\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is B.\n\nBut let me check another angle. Suppose there's a contradiction, so the conclusion can't hold?\n\nSuppose someone says Bob is not young. But according to the deductions, he must be young. So there is no contradiction. Therefore, the logic is sound.\n\nTherefore, the answer is B (False).\n</think>\n\nB) False\n\nStep-by-Step Explanation:\n1. **Bob is round** \u2192 By rule 5 (\"All round things are smart\"), Bob must be smart.\n2. **Bob is smart** \u2192 By rule 1 (\"All smart things are furry\"), Bob must be furry.\n3. **Bob is furry** \u2192 By rule 3 (\"If something is furry then it is young\"), Bob must be young.\n4. **Bob is young** \u2192 By rule 6 (\"If Bob is young then Bob is nice\"), Bob must be nice.\n\nThus, **Bob is nice**. The statement \"Bob is not nice\" is **false**, so the correct answer is **B) False**.",
