 the rabbit (point 10).\n   - No direct statement mentions the bear visiting the rabbit.\n\n2. **Conditional Rules Analysis:**\n   - **Rule 13:** If someone visits the rabbit, the rabbit visits the bear.  \n     - The squirrel visits the rabbit (point 10), so this triggers the rabbit\u2019s visit to the bear, which is already true (point 8).  \n     - This rule doesn\u2019t affect the bear\u2019s actions toward the rabbit.\n\n   - **Rule 12:** If someone is big and red, they chase the rabbit.  \n     - The cow is red and big (due to point 18 and inference from point 4). Thus, the cow chases the rabbit (inferred).  \n     - This inference doesn\u2019t link to the bear visiting the rabbit.\n\n   - **Rule 17:** If someone chases the rabbit, the rabbit is red.  \n     - The inferred cow chasing the rabbit (via Rule 12) forces the rabbit to be red (inferred). This doesn\u2019t impact the bear\u2019s visits.\n\n3. **No Direct or Indirect Links to Bear Visiting Rabbit:**\n   - No rule connects the bear\u2019s actions (chasing/eating the cow, being young) to visiting the rabbit.  \n   - The rabbit\u2019s visit to the bear (point 8) doesn\u2019t reciprocate via any rule.  \n   - No rule prohibits or mandates the bear visiting the rabbit.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - The statement \"The bear visits the rabbit\" is neither explicitly stated nor derivable from the given rules.  \n   - Since there\u2019s no contradiction but also no evidence, the correct answer is **Unknown (C)**.",
