squirrel?Not necessarily.The squirrel seeing the tiger doesn\'t imply the tiger sees the squirrel..\n\nHowever, the premise 15 says that if the mouse is red, then the tiger does not see the squirrel..\n\nIf the mouse were red, then the tiger must not see the squirrel.But does the tiger see the squirrel?The premises don\'t say the tiger sees the squirrel, except maybe through other deductions..\n\nWait, let\'s see.The tiger sees the mouse (statement 11).There\'s no direct mention of the tiger seeing the squirrel, but the squirrel seeing the tiger (from earlier deduction because the tiger needs the squirrel via premise 17 and thus premise 12 requires squirrel to see tiger)..\n\nSo if the squirrel sees the tiger, that\'s one direction, but the tiger\'s perception of the squirrel isn\'t stated..\n\nNow, let\'s see: if the mouse is red, then according to premise 15, the tiger does not see the squirrel.But is there any information about whether the tiger does see the squirrel?Not directly, unless there\'s a contradiction..\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can find the mouse\'s color through other premises.Let\'s see.\n\nThe mouse\'s color is tied to premise 14 and 15.Let\'s consider the possibilities.\n\nSuppose the squirrel is not green.Then, from premise 14 (since we know the squirrel eats the mouse from earlier), the mouse is not red.So the mouse can\'t be red.Then, if the mouse is not red, premise 15\'s condition (mouse is red) isn\'t met, so that doesn\'t impose any further constraints..\n\nAlternatively, if the squirrel is green, then premise 14\'s condition (squirrel is not green) is false, so the implication holds regardless of the mouse\'s color.So the mouse could be red or not..\n\nBut perhaps we can find the squirrel\'s color through other means.\n\nLooking at premise 17 again: If something is blue, then it needs the squirrel.The tiger is blue (statement 8), so it needs the squirrel (as we said before).The cow is blue (from premise 19, since it eats the mouse), so the cow must also need the squirrel (from premise 17).Wait, does the cow need the squirrel?.\n\nWait, the cow\'s needs aren\'t mentioned in the given premises except in premise 7 (squirrel needs the cow).So according to premise 17, since the cow is blue, the cow must need the squirrel.But the premises don\'t state that the cow needs the squirrel.Is that a problem?.\n\nWait, the premises are given as facts.So if the cow is blue, then according to premise 17, the cow must need the squirrel.But that\'s an additional fact we have to accept.So the cow needs the squirrel..\n\nBut then, if the cow needs the squirrel, then according to premise 12 (if something needs the squirrel, then the squirrel sees the tiger), the squirrel must see the tiger.Which we already had..\n\nSo, the cow needing the squirrel is an additional fact..\n\nNow, going back to the squirrel\'s color.Let\'s see if there\'s any direct info.The squirrel\'s color is not stated, but maybe through other premises.\n\nLooking at premise 14 again, but perhaps we can find another angle.\n\nSince the squirrel eats the mouse (from earlier deduction via premise 18), and if the squirrel is not green, then the mouse isn\'t red..\n\nBut if the mouse is not red, that\'s okay.But maybe the mouse\'s color is red?Let\'s see.\n\nSuppose the mouse is red.Then premise 15 would require that the tiger does not see the squirrel.But do we know if the tiger sees the squirrel?.\n\nAlternatively, the squirrel seeing the tiger (from premise 12) doesn\'t mean the tiger sees the squirrel.So maybe the tiger doesn\'t see the squirrel, which would be okay if the mouse is red..\n\nBut if the mouse is red, then premise 14 (if squirrel eats mouse and not green → mouse not red) would conflict, because if the mouse is red, then the antecedent (squirrel eats mouse and not green) would require that the mouse isn\'t red, which would be a contradiction..\n\nWait, let\'s parse that again..\n\nIf the squirrel doesn\'t eat the mouse, then the squirrel must be green. Alternatively, if the mouse is red, then the squirrel doesn\'t have to be green. If the squirrel is not green and eats the mouse, the mouse must be red. So, if the mouse isn\'t red, then the tiger doesn\'t have to see the squirrel. If the squirrel is green, then the mouse\'s color is not restricted. If something eats the mouse, it must be blue. Therefore, the squirrel is blue. The statement "The squirrel is not blue" is false. The cow is blue. Thus, the squirrel must be blue. The answer is B) False.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
