      "text": " C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but let me double-check the question again.\n\nThe question asks: is the statement \"Bob is not kind\" true, false, or unknown?\n\nIf there's no information that allows us to confirm or deny it, then it's unknown.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I missed a rule that makes it possible to conclude.\n\nWait, let me think of the contrapositive of premise 5: If something is not furry, then it is not kind. Wait no, premise 5 is \"All kind things are furry,\" which is equivalent to \"If something is kind, then it is furry.\" The contrapositive is \"If not furry, then not kind.\" So if something is not furry, then it is not kind. So that's a key point!\n\nAh! Wait a minute! This is crucial.\n\nWait, premise 5 is \"All kind things are furry.\" So that translates to: If something is kind (K), then it is furry (F). Symbolically: K \u2192 F.\n\nThe contrapositive is: If not furry (~F), then not kind (~K). So ~F \u2192 ~K. So if something is not furry, it cannot be kind.\n\nSo, if we can determine whether Bob is furry or not, then we can determine his kind status.\n\nWait, but how? Because if we can prove Bob is not furry, then by contrapositive, he must not be kind, making the statement \"Bob is not kind\" true.\n\nAlternatively, if he is furry, then we can't say for sure, because being furry is necessary but not sufficient for being kind.\n\nTherefore, the key is whether we can determine if Bob is furry or not.\n\nLet me see if we can determine Bob's furrieness.\n\nPremise 8: If something is furry (F), then it is green (G). F \u2192 G.\n\nBob is green (G is true). So, F \u2192 G is true, but that doesn't help us determine F. Because even if F is false (Bob not furry), the implication holds (F \u2192 G). So being green doesn't force him to be furry.\n\nAlternatively, if we can find a rule that requires Bob to be furry, then we can.\n\nLooking back:\n\nPremise 5's contrapositive: ~F \u2192 ~K. So if we can show Bob is not kind, then he must not be furry, but that's circular.\n\nAlternatively, is there any other rule that would require someone to be furry?\n\nPremise 5 only requires kind things to be furry, but there's no other premise that says, for instance, green things must be furry unless they're something else.\n\nHmm.\n\nWait, let me think of another angle.\n\nSuppose Bob is not furry (so ~F). Then by contrapositive of premise 5, he must not be kind (~K). Therefore, if he's not furry, then the statement \"Bob is not kind\" is true.\n\nIf he is furry (F), then he could be kind (K) or not (since F is necessary but not sufficient). So if F is true, then K could be true or false.\n\nTherefore, the only way for \"Bob is not kind\" to be false (i.e., Bob is kind) is if he is furry and there's no other constraints.\n\nHowever, if we can prove that Bob is not furry, then we can conclude \"Bob is not kind\" is true.\n\nSo the question now reduces to: Can we prove that Bob is not furry?\n\nLet me see.\n\nIf we can prove Bob is not furry, then done.\n\nHow?\n\nSuppose Bob is furry (F). Then, by premise 8, he is green (G), which he is. So that's okay.\n\nIs there any contradiction in assuming Bob is furry?\n\nLet me see.\n\nIf Bob is furry (F), then he could be kind (K) or not. If he is kind (K), then he must be furry (which is true), and since he's kind, by premise 7, if he were cold, he would be kind, but he's not cold. So that's okay.\n\nAlternatively, if he is furry and not kind, that's allowed because premise 5 only requires kind things to be furry, not the other way around.\n\nTherefore, there's no contradiction in Bob being furry.\n\nSo we can't prove Bob is not furry.\n\nTherefore, Bob could be either furry or not.\n\nIf he's not furry \u2192 \"not kind\" is true.\n\nIf he is furry \u2192 \"not kind\" could be true or false.\n\nTherefore, since there are scenarios where \"Bob is not kind\" is true and scenarios where it's false, the correct answer is \"unknown\" (C).\n\nWait, but let me check again. If Bob is