er is unknown (C). But the problem might not consider that, and expects us to proceed with the given steps.\n\nAlternatively, let's see if there's another path.\n\nSuppose the tiger is young. Then, by statement 15, it must be red. If the tiger is red, then by statement 10, it must eat the tiger itself (the tiger eating itself?), but that's another contradiction. Wait, if the tiger is red, then by statement 10, it would have to eat the tiger (itself again). So that's a problem.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger is young, so red, so it must eat the tiger (itself), which is impossible, so that would mean the tiger can't be young? That's conflicting.\n\nHmm, this is getting too tangled. Maybe I need to approach this systematically with a table or something.\n\nLet me list all entities: bald eagle, cow, dog, tiger.\n\nEach has certain attributes and actions.\n\nKnown facts:\n\n1. Bald Eagle (BE) eats cow (C).\n\n2. BE is young (Y) \u2192 so by 15, BE is red (R).\n\n3. Cow is nice (irrelevant?).\n\n4. Cow sees BE.\n\n5. Dog (D) eats BE.\n\n6. Tiger (T) eats C.\n\n7. T needs BE.\n\nNow, from BE's attributes:\n\nBE is Y and R.\n\nFrom BE being R:\n\n- By 10: R \u2192 eats T. So BE eats T.\n\n- By 14: R \u2192 eats BE (itself). Contradiction.\n\nBut let's proceed despite that.\n\nBecause BE eats T, by 9: if something eats T, then T is Y. So T must be Y.\n\nTherefore, T is Y \u2192 by 15, T must be R.\n\nIf T is R, then by 10 again, T would have to eat T itself (since T is red). Another contradiction.\n\nBut maybe the system allows for that? Or perhaps the problem is designed so that the contradictions are ignored, and we just follow the chain.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the fact that T needs BE (statement 7) can be considered. But how does needing relate to anything else?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the contradiction means that the premise cannot all be true, so the conclusion is unknown.\n\nHowever, in logic puzzles like this, usually contradictions between the statements are 