lors.But in logic terms, each statement is a separate fact, so Erin is both green and white.Gary is both green and red.\n\nNow, the conditionals.Let\'s parse them one by one.\n\nFirst conditional: If Erin is furry and Erin is blue then Erin is big.\n\nBut from the facts, Erin is green and white.The problem states Erin\'s colors as green and white, so Erin is not blue.Therefore, the antecedent (Erin being furry and blue) is false.So this implication is trivially true, but doesn\'t give us anything about Erin being big unless the antecedent is true.Since Erin isn\'t blue, this doesn\'t affect anything else.\n\nSecond: Red things are round.So any red object is round.Gary is red, so Gary is round.\n\nThird: If something is blue and big then it is white.Not sure yet.\n\nFourth: Red, furry things are white.So if something is both red and furry, then it must be white.\n\nFifth: If something is round then it is furry.Since Gary is red, he\'s round (from second conditional), so if Gary is round, then he must be furry.Wait, that\'s a key point.Since Gary is red, he is round (from second rule), so by the fifth rule, if round then furry.Therefore, Gary must be furry.But then, if Gary is red and furry, then according to the fourth rule (red, furry things are white), Gary must also be white.But from the given facts, Gary is green and red.Wait, but does that mean Gary can have multiple colors?The problem allows for multiple attributes, so maybe Gary is both green and red and white?But the original fact only states Gary is green and red.However, according to the fourth rule, if he is red and furry, then he must be white.So that would add white to his attributes.\n\nWait, but let\'s step through this again carefully.Let\'s track Gary\'s attributes step by step.\n\nGiven facts: Gary is green and red.\n\nFrom second conditional: Red things are round.So Gary is round.\n\nFifth conditional: If something is round, it is furry.Therefore, Gary must be furry.So that gives Gary another attribute: furry.\n\nNow, with Gary being red and furry, the fourth conditional says: red and furry things are white.Therefore, Gary must be white.\n\nSo now, Gary has green, red, white, and furry as attributes.But the problem didn\'t mention white for Gary, but according to the rules, he must be white now.So that\'s okay because the rules can add attributes based on conditions.\n\nSo, if Gary is furry, then the answer would be A) True.But let me check if there\'s any contradictions here.\n\nWait, let\'s check if any other rules might conflict.Let\'s see:.\n\nGary is green.The last conditional says if something is green then it is red.Well, Gary is already red, so that\'s okay.So that rule is satisfied.\n\nAnother rule: If something is furry and green then it is blue.Gary is furry and green, so according to this rule, he must be blue.Wait, that\'s a problem.Because Gary is currently green, red, white, furry, but if he must be blue because he\'s furry and green, then he must be blue as well.But the original fact didn\'t say he\'s blue, but according to this rule, he must be.So that would add blue to his attributes.So now Gary is green, red, white, furry, and blue.\n\nNow, check another rule: If something is blue and big then it is white.But do we know if Gary is big?Not directly yet.Let\'s see if any other rules can trigger that.\n\nWait, the first conditional was about Erin.Since Erin isn\'t blue, that doesn\'t apply.Let\'s see if any other rules might affect Gary\'s \'big\' status.Not sure yet.Maybe we can ignore that for now.\n\nBut let\'s see the other rules.The seventh rule: Blue, white things are furry.Gary is blue and white, so that\'s okay because he is already furry.So that\'s consistent.\n\nNow, is there any contradiction here?Let me check again.So far, the rules lead us to conclude Gary is furry, which would make the answer A) True.But let me check all steps again to be sure.\n\nStarting over for Gary:.\n\n1.Given: Gary is green and red.\n\n2.From "Red things a