n.\n\n17. If the bear visits the squirrel and the bear is round \u2192 then the bear sees the cow.\n\n18. If something eats the lion \u2192 then it sees the cow.\n\n19. If something is young and cold \u2192 then it eats the cow.\n\nWe need to determine if the cow is not red.\n\nLet me go step by step.\n\nFirst, the cow's color is not directly stated except through conditions.\n\nLooking at condition 11: If (X sees cow AND cow eats squirrel) \u2192 cow is red.\n\nWe know cow eats squirrel (fact 3). So the second part is true.\n\nDoes something see the cow?\n\nLooking at the given facts:\n\n- The squirrel sees the bear (fact 9).\n\n- The lion visits the cow (fact 8). But does visiting imply seeing? Not unless stated.\n\n- The bear eats the cow (fact 1). Eating might involve seeing, but unless specified, we can't assume.\n\nSo, unless there's an explicit \"sees\" between any entity and the cow, the first part of condition 11's antecedent isn't met.\n\nTherefore, for condition 11 to activate, we need something to see the cow.\n\nIs there any way that could happen?\n\nLooking at other conditions:\n\nCondition 16 says if something is red \u2192 it eats the lion.\n\nThe lion is red (fact 6), so the lion must eat the lion (itself).\n\nThen, condition 18: If something eats the lion \u2192 it sees the cow.\n\nSo, the lion eats the lion (due to being red), so the lion must see the cow.\n\nThus, the lion sees the cow becomes a fact derived from the conditions.\n\nTherefore, now we have that the lion sees the cow, and the cow eats the squirrel. Thus, condition 11's antecedent is satisfied (something [the lion] sees the cow and the cow eats the squirrel), so the conclusion is that the cow is red.\n\nTherefore, according to this, the cow must be red, so the statement \"The cow is not red\" is false \u2192 answer B.\n\nWait, but what about other conditions that might affect this?\n\nLet me check condition 12: If cow eats bear and bear sees cow \u2192 then bear is blue.\n\nThe cow eats the bear? The first fact says the bear eats the cow, so the cow doesn't eat the bear. Thus, the antecedent is false, so the condition doesn't apply.\n\nCondition 13: If something is blue and it sees the squirrel \u2192 squirrel is round.\n\nThe bear is blue (fact 2). Does the bear see the squirrel?\n\nThe squirrel sees the bear (fact 9), but that's squirrel's perspective. The bear's perspective isn't stated. Unless eating implies seeing, but we can't assume that. So the bear doesn't necessarily see the squirrel. Thus, condition 13's antecedent isn't met unless we can establish that the bear sees the squirrel.\n\nCondition 14: If something sees the squirrel \u2192 then squirrel eats the cow.\n\nDoes anyone see the squirrel? The squirrel sees the bear (fact 9). The bear might see the squirrel? Not stated. The cow or lion? Not stated. Thus, unless someone sees the squirrel, the antecedent isn't met. So unless there's another path, this condition doesn't activate.\n\nCondition 15: If lion sees cow AND cow visits squirrel \u2192 cow eats squirrel.\n\nWe have lion sees cow (from earlier), but does the cow visit the squirrel? The cow visits the lion (fact 5), but not stated to visit the squirrel. Thus, second part is false, so condition doesn't apply.\n\nCondition 17: If bear visits the squirrel and bear is round \u2192 then bear sees the cow.\n\nDoes the bear visit the squirrel? The squirrel visits the bear (fact 10), so that's squirrel going to bear, not the other way. So, bear doesn't visit the squirrel. Thus, antecedent is false.\n\nCondition 19: If something is young and cold \u2192 eats the cow.\n\nThe cow is young (fact 4), but is it cold? Not stated. Thus, can't apply unless we know the cow is cold, which we don't.\n\nTherefore, the only relevant condition leading to the cow's color is condition 11, which now activates, making the cow red. Thus, the cow is red, so the statement \"The cow is not red\" is false \u2192 answer B.\n\nHowever, let me check if there's