ar..\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the lion visiting the mouse is required for some condition?The problem doesn\'t state anything about the lion visiting the mouse unless triggered by point 13.So unless the mouse both likes and needs the lion, the lion wouldn\'t visit the mouse.But since the mouse does like the lion (point 7), if we can establish that the mouse needs the lion, then the lion would visit the mouse.But that doesn\'t help us determine whether the mouse needs the lion..\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another way.Let\'s think about the lion needing the bear (point 6).Since the lion needs the bear, then from point 9, the lion needs the lion.Then from point 10, needing the lion requires the lion to visit the lion.Then, does that have any effect on the mouse?Not directly..\n\nWait, let\'s see if the lion\'s visit to itself is necessary, but that\'s probably a side note.Let me check other points again..\n\nThe cow is rough (point 5).Since the cow is rough, from point 12, the cow needs the lion.Also, from point 14, the cow must visit the lion.So the cow needs the lion and visits the lion.Then, since the cow visits the lion, from point 8, the cow must be round.But wait, the cow\'s roundness isn\'t mentioned.The bear is round (point 1), but the cow\'s roundness isn\'t stated.However, according to point 8, if someone visits the lion, they must be round.The cow visits the lion (from point 14 and 12 leading to that), so the cow must be round.But the context says the cow is big and rough, but not round.Wait, this is a conflict.Because if the cow visits the lion (from point 14, since cow is rough), then by point 8, the cow must be round, but the context says the cow is big and rough, not necessarily round.Is that a problem?Or is it that the cow must be round because of the visit?.\n\nHmm, this might be a contradiction.The context says the cow is big and rough (points 4 and 5), but doesn\'t mention roundness.However, if the cow visits the lion (as per point 14 because t