      "text": " B) False.\n\nWait another angle: Let me check the rules again for any hidden contradictions.\n\nWait, rule 14 says \"Green people are furry.\"So green \u2192 furry.\n\nRule 10 says \"If someone is furry then they are green.\"So furry \u2192 green.\n\nTherefore, the two together mean that furry and green are equivalent.That is, someone is furry if and only if they are green.\n\nTherefore, if someone is furry, they must be green, and vice versa.\n\nTherefore, if Bob is cold \u2192 furry (from 15), then he must be green.\n\nWait a second, so that would mean Bob is green?But that contradicts my prior thought.\n\nWait, wait, so if furry and green are equivalent, then being furry implies green and vice versa.\n\nTherefore, if Bob is cold \u2192 furry, then he is furry \u2192 green. Thus Bob is green.\n\nWait, but that would make the answer A) True.\n\nWait now I'm confused. Where did I go wrong before?\n\nAh! Here's the mistake: I thought that if someone is furry, they must not be green, but actually, since they are equivalent, they must be both.\n\nWait, let me parse rules 10 and 14 again:\n\nRule 10: If someone is furry \u2192 they are green.\n\nRule 14: Green people are furry \u2192 Green \u2192 furry.\n\nTherefore, together, they form a biconditional: furry if and only if green.\n\nTherefore, anyone who is furry must be green, and anyone green must be furry.\n\nTherefore, if someone is cold \u2192 furry (rule 15), so they must be furry \u2192 green.\n\nTherefore, all cold people are green.\n\nTherefore, Bob is cold \u2192 furry \u2192 green. So Bob is green. Hence the answer would be A) True.\n\nWait, so my previous conclusion that Bob is not green was wrong because I made a mistake in interpreting the biconditional.\n\nWait, this is a critical point. Let me retrace:\n\nRule 10: furry \u2192 green.\n\nRule14: green \u2192 furry.\n\nTherefore, furry \u2194 green.\n\nTherefore, if someone is furry, they are green and vice versa.\n\nSo, if Bob is cold \u2