probably implies that an entity visits another entity different from itself.\n\nSo the dog cannot be the \"something\" in statement 21. Therefore, the antecedent remains false.\n\nHmm, so nothing so far leads to the lion being round.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a chain of reasoning where if the lion were round, then it must be big (statement 19). But we don't know if it's big. Alternatively, if the lion were not round, then there's no requirement from statement 19.\n\nAlternatively, is there any other way?\n\nWait, the cat is round (statement 7), so it's big (statement 19). Therefore, the cat is big. The dog is big (statement 12). The bald eagle is round (statement 2) so also big. So all three are big. The lion's bigness is unknown.\n\nWait, but could there be a situation where the lion must be round? Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, let me check the question again: the statement to evaluate is \"The lion is not round.\" So is that true? The options are True (A), False (B), or Unknown (C). So if we can prove the lion is round, then the statement is false (so answer B). If we can prove it's not round, then A. Otherwise, C.\n\nIs there any way to prove the lion is round?\n\nWait, let's think again about the dog. Since the dog is big (statement 12), by statement 16, it must eat the lion. So the dog eats the lion. Additionally, the dog eats the cat (statement 11). \n\nNow, let's look at statement 20: If something eats the cat and the cat eats the dog, then the cat eats the lion. The first part is true (the dog eats the cat), but the second part is false (the cat doesn't eat the dog), so the antecedent is false, so nothing to infer.\n\nStatement 21: If something visits the dog and eats the lion, then the lion is round. The \"something\" would need to both visit the dog and eat the lion. As before, the only one who eats the lion is the dog, who doesn't visit the dog. The only visitors to the dog are the bald eagle and the cat. The bald eagle doesn't eat the lion. The cat