l\'s antecedent is not met.Wait, the squirrel is red and chases the bald eagle.So the antecedent is true, so the consequent must be true.Therefore, the bald eagle must be green.But the bald eagle is stated to be blue.Therefore, there\'s a contradiction here, which might mean that some of the given statements can\'t be true at the same time, but perhaps the question is still answerable?.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem expects us to treat the bald eagle as blue despite the contradiction, and proceed, ignoring the inconsistency?Because sometimes in logic problems, you might have to go with the direct statements unless there\'s a contradiction that makes it impossible.Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in interpreting which statements are given as facts versus which are conditionals.Let me check again.\n\nThe first part lists facts: "The bald eagle chases the squirrel.The bald eagle is blue.The bald eagle likes the cow.The bald eagle likes the lion.The cow chases the lion.The cow is green.The cow likes the bald eagle.The cow likes the lion.The lion likes the cow.The squirrel chases the bald eagle.The squirrel is big.The squirrel is green.The squirrel is red.The squirrel sees the bald eagle.".\n\nThen the conditionals are the "If" statements.\n\nSo all the facts are given as true, and the conditionals are also rules that must hold.Therefore, the fact that the bald eagle is blue must be true, but the conditional says if the squirrel (who is red and chases the bald eagle) exists, then the bald eagle must be green.So this is a contradiction.Therefore, the set of premises is inconsistent..\n\nIn logic, if there\'s an inconsistency, then any statement can be inferred, but that\'s probably not the case here.Alternatively, maybe the problem expects us to ignore the contradiction and proceed with the given facts, assuming that the conditionals might have exceptions?Or perhaps the conditionals are only applicable when the facts don\'t contradict them?.\n\nAltern