le?.\n\nSuppose the lion does not see the cat.Is there any contradiction?.\n\nWell, let\'s see.The lion visits the cat (statement 11).If visiting doesn\'t imply seeing, then maybe that\'s okay..\n\nAlternatively, maybe statement 24.Let\'s think: the lion is visiting the bear (statement 10).The bear is young.If the lion were the one visiting the bear and the bear is young, then according to statement 24, the lion would have to see the cat.Wait, that\'s an important point!.\n\nStatement 24 says: If something is young and it visits the bear, then it sees the cat.\n\nWait, the structure is: If (X is young AND X visits the bear), then X sees the cat.\n\nIn this case, the lion visits the bear (statement 10).The lion\'s age isn\'t stated, but the bear is young (statement 1)..\n\nWait, the subject here is the visitor.The antecedent is about the visitor being young and visiting the bear..\n\nSo in statement 24, "something" (let\'s call it X) is young, and X visits the bear.Then X sees the cat..\n\nSo the lion is visiting the bear (statement 10).To apply statement 24, we need that the lion is young (X is young) and visits the bear..\n\nThe lion\'s age is not mentioned.The bear is young, but the lion\'s age isn\'t stated.So unless we can infer the lion is young, we can\'t apply this..\n\nWait, but the bear is young, and the bear visits the lion (statement 2).So the bear is young and visits the lion.But that\'s not visiting the bear..\n\nHmm, so for the lion visiting the bear, the lion would need to be young to trigger statement 24.Since the lion\'s age isn\'t stated, we can\'t assume that..\n\nTherefore, perhaps statement 24 isn\'t applicable here..\n\nWait another angle: the cat visits the lion (from statement 21 and 3).So the cat is visiting the lion..\n\nThen, if the cat visits the lion, then perhaps the lion sees the cat?But again, unless there\'s a rule, we can\'t assume that..\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a chain of reasoning.Let me think of all the entities and their relationships..\n\nLet me list all the entities and their attributes and actions:.\n\nBear:.\n- Young.\n- Visits lion.\n- Needed by the cat (cat needs bear).\n- Is visited by lion (statement 10).\n\nCat:.\n- Round.\n- Needs bear.\n- Is visited by lion (statement 11).\n- Visits lion (from statement 21: round → visits lion).\n- Is visited by rabbit (statement 16).\n\nLion:.\n- Cold.\n- Not green.\n- Round.\n- Does not need bear.\n- Sees bear (statement 9).\n- Visits bear (10).\n- Visits cat (11).\n- Visits rabbit (12).\n- Is seen by rabbit (15).\n- (From above, the cat visits the lion, so cat is visiting lion).\n\nRabbit:.\n- Green.\n- Rough.\n- Sees lion (15).\n- Visits cat (16).\n\nNow, let\'s see if the lion seeing the cat can be derived.\n\nLooking at the conditional statements again:.\n\nStatement 22: "If something sees the cat and the cat visits the lion, then it is not rough.".\n\nWe know the cat visits the lion (from above), so the second part is true..\n\nNow, if the lion sees the cat (i.e., the lion is the "something" that sees the cat), then the first part (lion sees the cat) would be true..\n\nTherefore, if the lion sees the cat, then since the cat visits the lion, the conclusion is that the lion is not rough..\n\nBut the lion\'s roughness isn\'t mentioned.The rabbit is rough (statement 14), but the lion\'s roughness isn\'t stated..\n\nHowever, if we can prove that the lion must be not rough, then that would be okay.But since we don\'t have any information about the lion\'s roughness, maybe that doesn\'t help..\n\nAlternatively, if the lion does not see the cat, then the antecedent of statement 22 is false, so the implication holds regardless..\n\nWait, but we need to see if the statement "lion does not see the cat" is true.To prove it\'s false, we need to find that the lion does see the cat..\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another path..\n\nWait, let\'s go back to the statement 24 again..\n\nStatement 24: "If something is young and it visits the bear,