d, but statement 13 says he can't be kind. So that's impossible. Therefore, Charlie must be smart. So that's solid.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is Charlie is white, so the statement is false. So B.\n\nWait, but the options are A) True, B) False, C) Unknown. The question is asking if \"Charlie is not white\" is true, so if Charlie is white, then the statement is false, so answer B.\n\nHmm. So I think that's the right path. But let me check again all the steps to be sure.\n\nAnother angle: Let's see if being white leads to any contradictions.\n\nCharlie is white, so from statement 9: okay, since he's rough. Also, from statement 10: he is red (since rough). So being both white and red? There's no rule against that except for the Gary's condition. But that's only for Gary. So that's fine.\n\nAlso, being smart (from above) and white is okay because smart\u2192white. So that's consistent.\n\nTherefore, all conditions are met.\n\nTherefore, the answer must be B) False.\n\nWait, but in the original problem statement, the question is \"is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Charlie is not white.\"\n\nSo if Charlie is white, then the statement \"Charlie is not white\" is false. Therefore the correct answer is B) False.\n\nHmm. So that's my conclusion. But let me check once more for any oversight.\n\nAnother possible point: The fact that Fiona is white (statement 6). Since Fiona is white, she must be rough (from statement 9). Which matches statement 5 (Fiona is rough). So that's consistent. Fiona is also kind (statement 4). But Fiona is rough and kind. Wait, but statement 13 says that rough and quiet things are not kind. But Fiona isn't necessarily quiet, right? Fiona's attributes are kind, rough, and white. So she's rough but not quiet. Therefore, statement 13 applies only to things that are both rough and quiet. So Fiona is okay.\n\nGary is nice (statement7). So by statement11, if nice \u2192 white. Therefore, Gary must be white. Then, since he's nice and white