n (given), it must visit the dog (condition 5: green\u2192visit dog). Therefore, the mouse visits the dog, and is round, so it must chase the tiger.\n\nDoes this cause any problem? The problem states that \"The rabbit does not chase the tiger,\" but the mouse chasing the tiger isn't prohibited. So the mouse chasing the tiger is acceptable. There's no contradiction here.\n\nTherefore, all conditions are satisfied by the mouse being big. Therefore, the statement \"The mouse is not big\" is false. So the answer is B) False.\n\nEarlier confusion was from misreading the \"mouse eats the tiger,\" but correcting that shows that the mouse must be big. Hence, the answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe mouse eats the tiger (given). According to the condition: \"If something eats the tiger then the tiger visits the mouse,\" the tiger must visit the mouse. This triggers the condition: \"If something visits the mouse then the mouse is big,\" so the mouse must be big. Therefore, the statement \"The mouse is not big\" is **false**.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
