 the squirrel.Therefore, the consequent must hold: the dog needs the mouse.\n\nNow, the dog needs the mouse?The original statements say the dog needs the cat (statement 6).Now, according to this, the dog also needs the mouse.That\'s a new fact.So the dog needs both the cat and the mouse?.\n\nOkay, so the dog needs the mouse now.Then, does that lead us anywhere?Let\'s see.\n\nThe fourth conditional says if something needs the mouse, it is rough.The dog now needs the mouse, so the dog must be rough.Therefore, the dog is rough.But the dog\'s roughness wasn\'t stated before.So the dog is rough.Then, since the dog is rough, from conditional 7, it must see the dog.Wait, the dog seeing itself?That\'s a bit odd, but logically, if an entity is rough, it must see the dog.So the dog must see itself?Hmm, but that\'s acceptable in logic.So the dog is rough and sees the dog (itself).Then, by conditional 1, if something sees the dog (the dog itself in this case), it must be red.Therefore, the dog is red?Wait, but the dog\'s color wasn\'t mentioned.But according to the first conditional, since the dog sees the dog (itself), it must be red.So the dog is red.\n\nWait, but the cat is also red.That\'s okay, multiple entities can be red.So the dog is red, rough, and not young (statement 5).Okay.\n\nNow, back to the squirrel.The squirrel needs the mouse (statement 12), so it\'s rough, red, young, and sees the dog..\n\nNow, the second conditional again: If something is red and needs the squirrel, then the squirrel likes the mouse.So if any entity is red and needs the squirrel, then the squirrel likes the mouse..\n\nWho needs the squirrel?The squirrel\'s needs are on the mouse (statement 12), but who needs the squirrel?The problem hasn\'t stated that anyone needs the squirrel except perhaps the mouse?No, the mouse needs the cat (statement 9).The squirrel is needed by no one mentioned except possibly itself?Not sure..\n\nAlternatively, maybe the dog now needs the mouse (from the third condi