he problem doesn't specify that. If the problem allows an entity to see itself unless stated otherwise, then the conclusion holds.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \"sees\" is always between different entities. But that's an assumption. The problem doesn't state that, so we can't assume that. We have to go strictly by the given information.\n\nSince the given logic leads us to the rabbit seeing itself, then the answer is B) False.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe rabbit is green (statement 7), so it is round (statement 19). All round things are young (statement 20), making the rabbit young. By statement 18 (\"If something is young then it sees the rabbit\"), since the rabbit is young, it must see the rabbit (itself). This directly contradicts the statement \"The rabbit does not see the rabbit,\" making it false. \n\n**Answer:** B) False",
